issues between these countries  because each country is built on a  different political system and has a  different  outlook qatar is a lot more pluralistic  in its approach it's a lot more open to  uh diversity and to different of  of opinions and deals with countries  from iran to israel in the past right  whereas the united arab emirates saudi  arabia  bahrain and egypt have consistently  pursued  more unilateral  policies have supported consistently  authoritarian regimes either military  ones like the  one in egypt or in in libya  or absolute monarchies and therefore  that hasn't changed so  if you are to follow the  analysis that is that the core of the  issue is  that difference of opinion particularly  when we're looking at it post arab  spring  then really all that's been happening is  you're putting a plaster on a gushing  wound  but that is enough at least for many  people in the region because  the stability that came from the gcc  even if it was  fractured behind the scenes was still  something that is worth  maintaining as opposed to having yet  another  divided and fractured segment of the  middle east and north africa  jamal thank you very much indeed for the  time being and now let's  bring in ramy kuri who's the director of  global engagement and a senior public  policy fellow at the american university  of beirut joins us via skype  from new york hopefully your line will  stay stable this time now listen i  wanted to ask you about the  where this puts the biden administration  and and its future relations  say with with iran does this complicate  the task for for biden and how to deal  with iran the fact that uh  uh that this result that this situation  has been is being resolved i don't think  so i think it probably is a positive  element  the the arab countries in the arab  region and the ones in the gulf and the  gcc  don't have a united position unified  position on iran some of them want to  use  forceful sanctions and threats and put  military options on the table other ones  want to have quiet diplomacy  but reducing the tensions between the  gcc countries and  qatar will definitely be a positive step  and the question of iran is going to  depend much more  on what people decide in washington and  maybe a little bit in israel  because israel drives a lot of  washington policy on this  that's going to be the determining  factor but qatar  has qatar and kuwait and all men have  good  relations with iran trading they talk  they're in  contact all the time and that's a very  positive thing  the the genesis of this conflict between  the or the blockade  uh because it's not really a two-way  conflict but the genesis was the  the saudis and the emiratis were  terrified after the arab uprisings of  210 to 11  about trends in the region democracy  voting leaders being thrown out open  media like jazeera and others  but they were also worried about the  united states starting to negotiate with  iran and the u.s pulling out of the  region  so having a sensible logical  political dialogue between iran and the  arab countries  is the only way to to to do this and  you've had some saudis and emiratis and  others  behind the scenes talking to to iranians  so we'll have to wait and see there's  also a new factor here which is some  countries possibly without naming any  because we don't know this is  speculation but some arab countries may  feel that their  links with israel may give them more  comfort in terms of dealing  with iran if the us is slowly  downgrading its role in the region  which it may or may not be but the links  with israel are also a new factor  that we have to put into this into this  situation but the real driver of this  whole  boycott issue was the united arab  emirates muhammad bin zayed  the crown prince and effective ruler of  the uae rather than  the saudis the bahrainis and the  egyptians went along with it because  they basically  gained a lot of material things and a  sense of sort of a role in the region  but the saudis are much more sensitive  to the damage  that has been done to their reputation  because of this because  the blockade because of the war in yemen  because of the internal factors that  they've  dealt with in terms of repression so the  saudis are much more sensitive to  international opinion the muratis are  much more forceful and decisive  and we don't know the relationship  between them right now it seems to be  evolving and we haven't heard anything  about  the emiratis opening their border or  doing anything so we have to really wait  to get more information about what is  the result of this  reconciliation or reduction in tension  opening of some borders  but it's a positive move everybody  should welcome it and people should  not repeat such foolhardy unilateral  blockades  that are based on political differences  that  translate into acts of almost irrational  political violence now tommy you  mentioned that in passing the  normalization of relations between some  gulf countries  with israel give us an idea of whether  this actually some of that might be tied  to this and in what in what way  well it might be the emiratis have  established  normal relations and some of them are  enthusiastic about economic ties of  course we don't know what the emirati  people feel about this the likelihood is  not all emiratis  are happy with this we know that around  the entire arab region  somewhere around 70 to 80 percent of the  population of the arab countries don't  want to normalize with israel until  israel  has a fair peace with the palestinians  then everybody's willing to normalize  the arab peace plan is based on normal  relations with israel which came out of  saudi arabia  and the arab summit years ago but we  don't know what average people around  the region think the normalization is a  is a is a logistical  mutually beneficial agreement between  individual leaders  in bahrain in the uae and morocco sudan  even the whole government isn't  behind the normalization or the steps  towards normalization the  military side wants to do it the  political side in sudan doesn't so  these are very delicate uh limited  depth uh logistical arrangements  rather than peace breaking out or  anything like that the  the interesting thing here also is the  announcement for this came out of kuwait  not out of washington as far as i can  tell those things may have changed in  the last 15 minutes  the united states wants to play this up  as a big deal and jared kushner is  trying to  take a lot of credit for it the  likelihood is that the omanis  and the kuwaitis did much most of the  heavy lifting in terms of  discussions and and how to get this  resolved  but again we just have to wait for the  details these things  are not completely clear right the  details are not completely clear right  now  and this may be a limited but  significant first step  towards reducing the tensions and  eventually getting back  to normal relations but normal relations  as your correspondence said  mean that different gulf countries have  different policies  they're different in their economic  weight their ideology  the nature of their leadership their  relationship to open societies and close  societies they're very very different  from each other  they've never had a strong unified  position on anything political really  and even they when they did say on  palestine they just broke it  because they thought they had things to  benefit so it has never been a major  political  bloc but it has been a functioning  uh technical uh collaboration among  these six  like-minded countries which started at  the moment in the 1970s  when i was i'm old enough to remember  when it started and it was very much  seen as a rich  arab rich persons club to protect the  wealth of the arab  gulf region that suddenly boomerang the  mushroomed in the 70s when oil prices  increased and people saw the gcc as a  way for the gulf countries to  try to protect their wealth and  and it never had any real political  weight and has much less now there's  another danger here  the uae and the saudis especially the  uae may suffer what the trump  administration  has suffered which is a drop in trust  internationally people might say well i  can't make a deal with this country  because they'll just break it  unilaterally  like trump did with different you know  climate change and  other things and the emiratis and saudis  just unilaterally broke  the relations with qatar boycotted them  started the war in yemen  and this kind of unilateral impulsive  often violent  behavior whether it's military or just  political violence  this is a problem for many countries  because others will look twice before  making  agreements with them in the future ramey  corey thank you very much indeed  for your analysis
 

